    "# Lecture 18: Exception Handling in Python\n",
    "\n",
    "In this lecture, we will learn how to make Python programs more robust by handling errors gracefully.\n",
    "\n",
    "## Goals\n",
    "By the end of this lecture, students should be able to:\n",
    "- explain what an exception is\n",
    "- use `try` and `except`\n",
    "- handle multiple exception types\n",
    "- raise exceptions with `raise`\n",
    "- understand how exceptions behave inside functions\n",
    "- use `finally` for cleanup\n",
    "- define a simple custom exception\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "7a126a31",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 1. Why do we need exception handling?\n",
    "\n",
    "When a program receives unexpected input or reaches an invalid operation, Python raises an **exception**.\n",
    "If we do not handle it, the program stops immediately.\n",
    "\n",
    "Let's first see a few common examples.\n"
